 "notes": "27 stations: Nos. 4-29 plus 7B, read from lines 183-217. Every row is on one printed line. The fragments keep the leading spaces because where an asterisk sits is what tells you its year.\n\nMarks: the asterisks sit under three columns headed \"1879, '80, '81.\" (line 182), at text columns 5, 10 and 15. In `marks` I give the asterisks as printed, then the years they fall under in square brackets. I added the brackets from the column heading; they are not printed in the row. The 1881 column has 12 asterisks (Nos. 5, 7, 8, 10, 14, 15, 16, 19, 24, 26, 27, 28), which matches the text. The plain-text layout may have shifted a column slightly, so it is worth checking against a scan, especially the single asterisks for Nos. 11, 16, 17, 19 and 24.\n\nOther column: this is the light's height in feet. Only row 4 prints \"230 ft.\"; every row after it uses a ditto mark (\"). I kept that as printed. Fair Isle's height is printed as \"?\".\n\nCoast: Nos. 4-7B have no sub-heading of their own, so I gave them the table heading \"East Coast of Scotland\" (line 180). They are the Shetland stations. Nos. 8-14 are under \"Orkney\" and Nos. 15-29 under \"Mainland\". Rows under Orkney print no county, so their place is null.\n\nKeepers: I dropped the full stop that ends each row (\"Robert Burnett.\" becomes \"Robert Burnett\"). Abbreviations are kept (\"Robt. S. Ritson\", \"D. MacDonald\"). Fair Isle's keeper is printed \"--Lawrence\". The dash seems to stand for an unknown forename, or Lawrence may be a surname, so I kept it.\n\nNo light types (L.H./L.V.) or light characters are printed in this list.\n\nPrinted forms to check, all copied as printed:\n- \"North Uist, Shetland\": North Uist is in the Outer Hebrides, not Shetland. This is almost certainly a misprint or OCR slip for North Unst (Muckle Flugga).\n- \"Whalsey Skerries\": usually spelt Whalsay.\n- \"Neven Kerr\": possibly Nevin.\n- \"Chanonry Point, Elgin\": Chanonry is in Ross-shire (Fortrose), not Elgin.\n- \"Cromarty, East Cromarty\": an odd county form.\n- \"Montroseness, Aberdeen\": Montrose is in Forfarshire, not Aberdeenshire.\n- \"Girdleness\" and \"Inch Keith\": printed as shown, as one word and as two words.\n- Hoy Sound (High) at 315 ft: this looks high and may be a misprint (Graemsay High is roughly 115 ft). Check it against Findlay.",
